From 1703e4c8967c00554a9ebb1c446b7d87e4c38373 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <noreply@invalid>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 22:03:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 72/81] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, closing the framebuffer is
 releasing the window

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Horizon WSI backend)
Why: patch 0070 established that only the swapchain owning the window's
 registration may call framebufferClose, and documented the rule in a
 comment naming two callers. There were three. The third was
 vkCreateSwapchainKHR's fail_no_chain path, which held no lock and
 tested no ownership - the exact shape of the defect 0070 had just
 fixed. It was unreachable, because fb_created was set on
 wsi_horizon_init_fallback's last line and so was never true there, but
 unreachability is not a property the next `goto fail_no_chain` is
 obliged to preserve, and the comment beside it asserted the invariant
 held rather than checking it. In the same function, two error paths
 called framebufferClose raw - unlocked, untested, and in the one place
 the header says that is a window operation - and then returned into a
 teardown that called nwindowReleaseBuffers on the window
 framebufferClose had already disconnected.
Evidence: cross build. aarch64-none-elf-gcc from
 ghcr.io/d3fau4/nx-dev, the exact command line meson records for this
 file in build/mesa-nvk/compile_commands.json with -fsyntax-only: no
 diagnostics. NOT verified on hardware - the newest hardware evidence
 for this file remains run 20, which predates this patch.
Upstream: no - this file is the Horizon backend itself.

The restructure is a subtraction. wsi_horizon_release_window already
took the surface lock and already tested ownership; the framebuffer
close now happens inside that test, because closing it and releasing
the buffers are both window operations and both halves of the same act.
Teardown and the creation failure path each call release_window and
nothing else, so neither can take the wrong lock, skip the test, or
close a framebuffer that is no longer theirs - there is nowhere left to
do it from. Three call sites become two, and the two that remain are
the two the comment always claimed.

What was prose is now checked. wsi_horizon_close_framebuffer asserts
that the surface lock is held and that the swapchain it is given is the
owner. Both hold at eviction, where the winner closes the loser's
framebuffer while the loser is still recorded as owner, and at release.

fb_created is set the moment framebufferCreate succeeds rather than at
the end of init_fallback, so it means "a Framebuffer exists and somebody
must close it" for the whole of its life. The two raw closes go with it,
and so does the double release they caused.

Two smaller things the same reasoning reaches:

- The eviction path called nwindowReleaseBuffers straight after
  framebufferClose, which had already released the window. On the copy
  fallback that second call is a release of a disconnected window on the
  ordinary recreate path, and libnx answering it with a failure logged a
  warning about a call with nothing to do. It now runs only when no
  framebuffer was closed, which is exactly the zero-copy case where it
  is the only release and must stay.

- Eviction reset every slot of the evicted swapchain from ACQUIRED to
  FREE, under the comment "the disconnect took them". That is true of
  BufferQueue slots and only the zero-copy path has any. On the copy
  fallback those entries are our own staging images: the BufferQueue
  never held them, and marking one FREE would offer the next acquire an
  image the application may still be rendering into, while Mesa's
  wsi_image::acquired still says it is held. The reset is now zero-copy
  only, which is the same condition wsi_horizon_release_slots already
  applies. Not a live defect - presentable is cleared first, so every
  acquire on an evicted swapchain returns VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR
  before reaching the free-slot search - so this is a guard against a
  future caller, not a fix for an observed failure.

Found in review of PR #9.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011kZa1YF3mTLVEZrLyzgsVr
